    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm having an issue with some walnut I'm working with for a cabinet project. I'm cutting dovetails by hand, but I keep getting these small splinters and tear-outs right at the tips of the pins. It's ruining the fit. Any idea what I might be doing wrong with my chisel or my grain direction?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It sounds like your chisel angle and grain direction could be the problem. Make sure to hold the chisel at a 45-degree angle relative to the work surface and always chip away from your body. Also, always work with the grain to reduce the risk of tearing out. Consider using a V-bit or a dovetail saw to guide your cuts, which might provide more control over the final result. Lastly, don't forget to use sharp, high-quality hand tools as they can make a significant difference in achieving clean cuts.",
