    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I've definitely been keeping them sharp, I strop them every few minutes, but it's like the more I focus on the grain, the more it seems to fight back in some spots. It's not consistent across the whole board. Is there anything else involving moisture or wood density that could cause these random little breakouts during the paring process?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Wood density and moisture content can significantly affect the ease of cutting and the quality of your dovetails. If you've only been considering the grain direction and not the wood's density, you might definitely be able to run into inconsistencies, especially with hardwoods like walnut. Pre-drying the wood by storing your cabinet pieces in a climate-conscious environment for at least 48 hours can help. This way, the moisture content is even throughout, reducing the chance of uneven splits and splinters. Moreover, avoid working with the wood directly after a rain or while it's back in the adventures of springtime condensation. Patience is key; let the wood acclimate. Side grain is also a more forgiving direction to cut dovetails, as it's generally less prone to tear-outs than end grain. If you're inconclusive, investing in a moisture meter will help you assess the wood's condition more accurately, ensuring a more consistent paring process.",
      "source": "target",
